title: Marriage – God’s Plan for Man and Woman
description: One flesh in the beginning, one flesh under the Law, one marriage supper at the end of all things: a study of marriage in the King James Bible.
output: lessons/doctrine/doctrine-week-10-marriage-gods-plan.html

# Doctrine Week 10. Converted from the old one-page lesson, section for section (I-V plus the two feature blocks,
# "Warnings & Duties" and the closing), in the author's order.

=== Introduction
--- Marriage: God’s Plan for Man and Woman [title]
overline: Adult Sunday School · Faith Baptist Church of Plymouth County · Week 10
heading: Marriage: God’s Plan for Man and Woman
sub: One flesh in the beginning, one flesh under the Law, one marriage supper at the end of all things
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the study. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

--- One flesh
big: Genesis 2:24

=== I. In the beginning
--- In the beginning
p: God, having created all things (light, day and night, the heavens and the earth, dividing the waters from the land, the creatures and their habitations) made man from the dust of the earth. Genesis 1 gives us the overview of creation; Genesis 2 slows down to detail the creation of man and woman.

--- Made in His image
fig: Made in His image, given dominion | Genesis 1:26-28
fig: Formed of the dust, the breath of life | Genesis 2:7-8

--- An help meet for him
group: An help meet for him | Adam named every living creature, but among them no help meet was found for him, so God made one. | Genesis 2:15-20
group: Bone of my bones, flesh of my flesh | Woman was taken out of Man, and so a man leaves father and mother to become one flesh with his wife. | Genesis 2:21-25

--- The purpose of mankind
p: From the beginning, having created man in Their image, a picture of Christ, through whom all things were made, God created woman from man as a help meet: a helper for all things he might do. Because of this, a man leaves his father and mother and cleaves unto his wife, that they be one flesh.
p: This fulfills the command of the Lord: be fruitful and multiply. Replenish the earth, meaning to fill it. Subdue the earth, exercising dominion and stewardship of creation. This was the purpose of mankind, in communion with the Lord.
callout-honest: And God saw His creation, that it was good, and rested from His works. But it wasn’t to last: Genesis 3.

=== II. God’s design for marriage
--- God’s design for marriage
lead: One man, one woman, one flesh: a lifelong covenant with a purpose bigger than the two people in it.
fig: The foundation of marriage | Genesis 2:24 | God’s original design.
fig: God’s provision against fornication | 1 Corinthians 7:2
fig: Marriage is honourable in all | Hebrews 13:4

--- The great mystery
group: The great mystery: Christ and the Church | Marriage as a picture of the gospel: the husband as head, as Christ is head of the Church. | Ephesians 5:22-25
group: Exhortations to godly marriage | | Titus 2:4-5; 1 Peter 3:1-7

=== III. God’s view of divorce
--- God’s view of divorce
lead: The {Lord} hates putting away, but Scripture also shows how He deals with the hardness of men’s hearts.
rev: The {Lord} hates putting away | Malachi 2:16
rev: The Sermon on the Mount | Matthew 5:32

--- The Mosaic concession and the Lord’s command
group: The Mosaic concession | An Old Testament provision, hedged with a heavy consequence for remarrying a former spouse. | Deuteronomy 24:1-4
group: The Lord’s command to the married | | 1 Corinthians 7:10-15

--- What God hath joined together [title]
overline: Jesus’ full answer
heading: What God hath joined together
sub: The Pharisees came tempting Jesus, asking whether it was lawful to put away a wife “for every cause.” Jesus took them back past Moses, past the Law, all the way to Eden, to what marriage was always meant to be.

--- No more twain
big: Matthew 19:6

--- Because of the hardness of your hearts
scripture: Matthew 19:8-9 | mark: because of the hardness of your hearts

--- Warnings and duties
group: Marrying the put away | A woman who puts away her husband and marries another committeth adultery, and whosoever marries her that is put away committeth adultery. | Mark 10:12; Luke 16:18
rev: Bound so long as he liveth | Romans 7:2
rev: Worse than an infidel | 1 Timothy 5:8

=== IV. Adultery under the Law
--- Adultery under the Law
lead: The Law demanded death for both parties, without exception.
rev: A capital offense, both parties | Leviticus 20:10
group: So shalt thou put away evil from Israel | | Deuteronomy 22:22-24

--- The woman taken in adultery [title]
overline: Grace that upholds the Law
heading: The woman taken in adultery
sub: The scribes and Pharisees brought a woman caught in the very act, testing Jesus: Moses commanded she be stoned. What sayest thou? Jesus stooped down and wrote on the ground.

--- Without sin
big: John 8:7; John 8:11

--- The standard upheld
p: The Law demanded death for both parties, yet only the woman was brought. Jesus did not abolish the Law; He exposed the accusers’ own guilt. His verdict was not cheap grace, “go, and sin no more,” but mercy that upheld the standard while extending redemption. Grace does not erase the Law; it fulfills it.

=== V. The marriage supper of the Lamb
--- The marriage supper of the Lamb
lead: Every earthly marriage points forward to this: Christ the Bridegroom, and His Church, the bride, prepared and adorned.
group: Blessed are they which are called | | Revelation 19:7-9
group: Washed in the blood of the Lamb | The bride, the redeemed of all ages: a great multitude in robes of white. | Revelation 7:9; Revelation 7:13-14
group: King of Kings, and Lord of Lords | The Bridegroom: Christ the King. | Revelation 19:11-16
group: Prepared as a bride adorned | New Jerusalem, shown as the bride, the Lamb’s wife. | Revelation 21:2; Revelation 21:9-11

=== Marriage: from Eden to eternity
--- Marriage: from Eden to eternity
p: The Bible opens with a wedding: one man, one woman, one flesh in the garden of God. The Bible closes with a wedding: the Lamb and His bride, the redeemed of all ages, clothed in white, gathered at the marriage supper of the Lamb.
p: Every earthly marriage is a shadow of that eternal union. Christ is the Bridegroom. The Church is His bride. He gave Himself for her, He is coming for her, and He will present her to Himself: holy, without blemish, in fine linen, clean and white.
big: Ephesians 5:32
